\subsection{Measures}
\textbf{Pre-test}: 15 multiple-choice questions covering Newtonian gravity, special relativity kinematics, and basic differential geometry (metric, geodesic concepts); plus a 10-item spatial reasoning test (Cronbach's $\alpha=0.82$ in pilot).  
\textbf{Post-test}:  
\textit{Conceptual understanding}: 20 multiple-choice items targeting equivalence principle, null geodesics, role of acceleration vs.\ spacetime curvature, and common misconceptions (e.g., ``photons have mass''). Open-ended prompts ask students to explain the deflection mechanism in their own words (scored via a rubric with inter-rater reliability $\kappa>0.75$).  
\textit{Problem-solving}: Calculate the deflection angle for a star near the solar limb given $M_\odot$ and $R_\odot$, and for a generic compact object.  
\textit{Transfer}: Qualitative explanation of gravitational time dilation in an accelerating frame using the equivalence principle, assessed with a 5-point conceptual rubric.  
\textbf{Cognitive load}: NASA-TLX questionnaire with six subscales (mental demand, physical demand, temporal demand, performance, effort, frustration) on a 1--21 scale; overall weighted score computed.  
\textbf{Learning analytics}: From the simulation logs, we extract dwell time on each layer, number of toggles, and sequence of interactions.
=======
\subsection{Measures}
\textbf{Pre-test}: 15 multiple-choice questions covering Newtonian gravity, special relativity kinematics, and basic differential geometry (metric, geodesic concepts); plus a 10-item spatial reasoning test (Cronbach's $\alpha=0.82$ in pilot, Rasch person separation reliability = 0.85).  
\textbf{Post-test}:  
\textit{Conceptual understanding}: 20 multiple-choice items targeting equivalence principle, null geodesics, role of acceleration vs.\ spacetime curvature, and common misconceptions (e.g., ``photons have mass''). Item-level analysis showed acceptable internal consistency (Cronbach's $\alpha=0.78$). Open-ended prompts ask students to explain the deflection mechanism in their own words (scored via a rubric with inter-rater reliability $\kappa=0.78$).  
\textit{Problem-solving}: Calculate the deflection angle for a star near the solar limb given $M_\odot$ and $R_\odot$, and for a generic compact object. Items were calibrated using item response theory (IRT) with discrimination parameters ranging from 0.82 to 1.24.  
\textit{Transfer}: Qualitative explanation of gravitational time dilation in an accelerating frame using the equivalence principle, assessed with a 5-point conceptual rubric (Cronbach's $\alpha=0.81$).  
\textbf{Cognitive load}: NASA-TLX questionnaire with six subscales (mental demand, physical demand, temporal demand, performance, effort, frustration) on a 1--21 scale; overall weighted score computed (Cronbach's $\alpha=0.89$).  
\textbf{Learning analytics}: From the simulation logs, we extract dwell time on each layer, number of toggles, and sequence of interactions.
