\section{Discussion}

Our findings provide strong empirical support for the efficacy of scaffolded simulation-based instruction in upper-division physics education. The significant conceptual gains observed in the Simulation-enhanced group ($d=1.41$ vs. Control) align with cognitive load theory predictions, suggesting that the progressive layering of mathematical abstractions effectively manages intrinsic cognitive load while supporting germane processing. The finding that the Simulation group outperformed the Heuristic-only group ($d=0.89$) indicates that the interactive visualization component provides added value beyond the heuristic derivation alone—likely by grounding abstract mathematical concepts in tangible physical experience.

The ablation study results are particularly noteworthy: the progressive revelation condition yielded substantially larger effects than the static overlay condition ($d=0.74$ for conceptual gains, $d=1.21$ for cognitive load reduction). This supports the theoretical claim that temporal sequencing of complexity is a critical design feature, not merely the presence of interactive elements. The pattern of misconception reduction—particularly the near-elimination of M2 ("mass essential for bending") in the progressive subgroup—suggests that the simulation helps students internalize the equivalence principle as a conceptual bridge rather than a mere analogy.

These results resonate with prior work in physics education research. The effectiveness of the heuristic derivation ($d=0.60$ vs. Control) replicates findings by Baily and Finkelstein (2015) regarding simplified approaches to quantum interpretations, while the superior performance of the simulation-enhanced approach extends the multimedia learning principles of Mayer (2021) to the domain of general relativity. Our work advances beyond prior simulation-based studies (e.g., Koerber et al., 2019) by providing rigorous empirical validation and demonstrating transfer to related concepts like gravitational time dilation.

