\subsection{Assessment Instruments and Scoring}
\label{subsec:instruments}

\textbf{Light Deflection Concept Inventory (LDCI).} A 20-item multiple-choice instrument was developed and validated for this study, assessing conceptual understanding of gravitational light deflection. Items target the nature of null geodesics, role of acceleration vs. spacetime curvature, weak-field approximation, and common misconceptions (e.g., photons "falling" due to mass, confusion between coordinate systems). The LDCI demonstrated strong internal consistency (Cronbach's $\alpha = 0.87$) in pilot testing with $N=30$ physics majors. Each item is scored 0 (incorrect) or 1 (correct), yielding a maximum score of 20.

\textbf{Problem-Solving Assessment.} A 10-point quantitative assessment requiring calculation of the deflection angle for a ray grazing the Sun ($M_\odot$, $R_\odot$) and for a generic compact object. Scoring follows a detailed rubric with partial credit for correct intermediate steps (e.g., identifying relevant equations, proper unit conversion).

\textbf{Transfer Assessment.} A 5-point qualitative assessment measuring application of concepts to gravitational time dilation, with responses scored using a validated conceptual rubric (inter-rater reliability $\kappa = 0.82$).

\textbf{NASA-TLX Cognitive Load Survey.} The NASA Task Load Index questionnaire was administered immediately after the post-test, measuring six subscales (mental demand, physical demand, temporal demand, performance, effort, frustration) on a 1–21 scale. Overall workload score is computed as the weighted sum of subscale ratings.

\textbf{Conceptual Gains.} Normalized learning gain is defined as $g = (\mathrm{post} - \mathrm{pre})/(1 - \mathrm{pre})$ for the LDCI, where scores are expressed as proportions of maximum possible score.

