\subsection{Participant Demographics and Randomization}
\label{subsec:demographics}

The 120 participants were second-year undergraduate physics majors enrolled at the University of Automated Discovery, with a mean age of $20.4 \pm 1.1$ years and 44\% female representation. Participants were recruited from three sections of a classical mechanics course that had covered special relativity but not general relativity. Block randomization by class section was used to assign participants to the three experimental conditions, ensuring balanced distribution across cohorts. Prior physics exposure was controlled by stratifying assignment based on performance in the preceding mechanics exam (mean score $82.3 \pm 7.2$). No participants were excluded based on the exclusion criteria (prior formal coursework in general relativity or differential geometry). Attrition was minimal: 118 participants completed all assessments (98.3\% compliance rate), with two participants in the Control group withdrawing due to scheduling conflicts.

\subsection{Baseline Equivalence}
\label{subsec:baseline}

A one-way ANOVA on the foundational GR and spatial reasoning pre-test confirmed no significant between-group differences ($F(2,117)=0.42$, $p=0.66$), ensuring that any post-treatment effects are not attributable to pre-existing knowledge disparities. Pre-test scores on the Light Deflection Concept Inventory (LDCI) likewise showed no significant differences ($F(2,117)=0.31$, $p=0.73$), with means of Control $6.2$ (SD $2.1$), Heuristic-only $6.4$ (SD $1.9$), and Simulation-enhanced $6.1$ (SD $2.3$). All subsequent analyses therefore compare raw post-test scores and normalized learning gains, defined as $\langle g\rangle = (post - pre)/(max - pre)$.

